// EXPECT-ERROR: Undefined trait: Any
// Design guard: `Any` is an ordinary trait name in trait position.
//
// A parent trait is resolved exactly like any other name: if nothing declares
// it, the program is rejected. `Any` gets no free pass.
//
// This guards against reintroducing `SemaUtils.isBuiltinTrait`, which answered
// `true` for the spellings `Any` and `mutable` and made three lookups skip the
// declaration entirely -- `validateTraitName`, `flattenedTraitMethods` and
// `orderedTraitMethods`. Under that rule `trait Bar Any` compiled with `Any`
// undeclared, and a `given` implementing `Bar` did not have to implement the
// parent at all, because the parent's method list came back empty. A user who
// declared their OWN `trait Any { m(self) Int; }` then found that the bound
// `T Any` still granted no methods -- the same spelling meant one thing in a
// bound and another in a parent list.
//
// The rule is now uniform: the name is looked up, and an unbound name is an
// error. Compare `given_undefined_trait_error.koral`, which asserts the same
// message for a user-chosen name.

trait Bar Any {
    n(self) Int;
};

type Demo(v Int);

given Demo as Bar {
    public n(self) Int = 1;
};

let main() Int = {
    return 0;
};
